Jae,

This shouldn't take so loong. I would kill the job and rerun with option

inla(...., verbose = TRUE)

and see what is going on. My guess, is that it is using all the time
getting the last digits of a very flat posterior right. You'll see this
easily in the output. Also it will say 'switch to stupid search mode' or
something, which is also a bad sign if it stays to long there. I guess
there are model components, like a rw2, that is essentially a linear
trend, which creates trouble. More informative priors may also help. You
need to enable verbose=TRUE to see what is going on.

About this num.threads:   inla.setOption(...) is the same as
inla(...,num.threads=...).   Also the runtime system behind OpenMP does
not allow us to determine how many threads that should be used, only the
maximum number of threads. The 'run-time system' controls the details.
The inla-program have different strategies for this as well, depending
on the size of the model and the number of hyperparameters, so...

If this model still takes awfull ammount of time, let us know and I'll
rerun it here.

H

